.TH CLOG_CREATE_FROM_FILE_MP 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_create_from_file_mp, clog_create_from_file \- create a file-backed logger with optional log rotation
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog clog_create_from_file_mp(const char *path, clog_level_t min_level,
                    const clog_rotation_cfg_t *cfg,
                    const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg,
                    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline clog clog_create_from_file(const char *path,
                    clog_level_t min_level,
                    const clog_rotation_cfg_t *cfg,
                    const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new logger that writes to the file at path.  A file that does
not exist is created with mode 0644, narrowed by the umask of the
process, and a file that exists is opened for append.  For an existing
file the function finds the current size, asking fstat(2) first and
falling back to an lseek(2) to the end of the file, so the thresholds
of size rotation are right for an append to a log that already exists.
If neither method gives the size and cfg->size_rotation_enabled is
true, the call fails and returns CLOG_NULL instead of silently
starting the byte counter from zero, which would let the file grow well
past cfg->max_file_size before its first rotation.

Log rotation is on only when cfg is not NULL, and size rotation and
time rotation can be turned on separately.

With rotation on, the logger opens the directory of path and keeps it
open for its whole life.  It opens the file, and does every rename,
listing, compression and deletion of a rotation, relative to that
directory, so a relative path keeps naming the same file after the
process changes its working directory with chdir(2).

Size rotation triggers after the write that takes the total bytes
written to cfg->max_file_size or past it, and it is checked after each
write.

Time rotation triggers on the first write once
cfg->rotation_interval_us microseconds have passed.  The check works on
whole seconds, so an interval that is not a whole number of seconds
rotates at the first whole second at or after its end.  The time counts
from the last rotation.  Before the first rotation of the logger, when
the file already holds data, it counts from the moment that file began:
the stamp of the newest rotated generation on disk, since the rotation
that made that generation also created the current file.  Without such
a generation it is the birth time of the file where the system reports
one (statx(2) on Linux, stat(2) on FreeBSD and macOS), and otherwise
(on OpenBSD, for example) the last modification time.  On FreeBSD,
changing the modification time to an earlier moment also moves the
birth time to that moment.  When the file is empty or new, the
interval counts from the creation of the logger, so a process that
runs for less than the interval, or restarts often, still rotates.  A
starting moment in the future counts as the creation of the logger.
The interval is checked before each write.
If the wall clock steps back to a moment before the start of the
interval, the interval starts again at that moment, so the next time
rotation comes one interval after the step and not when the clock
reaches its earlier reading again.

A rotation that fails is tried again on a later write, at least one
second after the failure, and a wall clock that steps back does not
make that wait longer than one second.

When a rotation fires, the library first creates the next live file
under a private name, <path>.tmp followed by 11 random hex digits, with
O_CREAT, O_EXCL and O_NOFOLLOW, so the file is always a new regular
file that the library made.  It then renames the current file to
<path>.<YYYYMMDDHHMMSS> with a rename that never replaces an entry of
that name, and renames the new file onto <path>.  Both renames are
atomic, and the rotation never opens <path> or a rotated name.  A link,
a FIFO or a file that another user puts at <path> while a rotation runs
is replaced by the new live file, so it never receives a record and
never blocks the rotation.  A process that stops between creating the
private file and renaming it leaves that file behind empty; the next
rotating open of path removes such an empty regular file, and nothing
else at a name of that shape.  A second rotation inside the same second
takes a collision suffix, _0001, _0002 and so on, zero-padded to four
digits, and a number above 9999 takes the digits it needs, such as
_10000.  Every rotation takes a name above every rotated name before
it: a later rotation inside the same second takes the next number,
never a name that a deletion freed.  A logger that starts in a
directory which already holds rotated generations (see below) numbers
its rotations above the newest of them, so a process that restarts
inside the same second continues the sequence.  If the clock reads an
earlier second than the newest rotated name, the rotation keeps the
stamp of that name and takes the next number.  As with logrotate(8),
the new live file gets the permission bits of the file it replaces,
whatever the umask, and the owner and the group of that file where the
process is allowed to give them.  If the process cannot give it the
group, the group bits are cleared so that no other group gains access.
A log that its operator restricted, to 0600 or 0640 for example, stays
restricted in every generation that way, and each \&.gz file takes the
bits of its source.  Once the new live file holds <path>, the oldest
rotated files past cfg->max_rotated_files are deleted.  A rotation that
fails never deletes a rotated file on its own account.

A directory entry is a rotated generation of the log only when its name
matches <path>.<YYYYMMDDHHMMSS>[_N][.gz] exactly, where N is four
zero-padded digits or a number from 10000 to 999999999, and the entry,
looked at without following a link, is a regular file whose owner is
the effective user of the process or the owner of the live file, and
whose timestamp is no more than 30 days above both the clock and the
newest name the logger made.  Naming rotations, deleting, compressing
and recovering compressions act on generations only.  Any other entry
is never opened, compressed, renamed or deleted, and takes no place
among the cfg->max_rotated_files kept.  That covers an unrelated file
whose name starts with a timestamp of a similar shape, a link, a FIFO,
a file owned by another user and a name stamped far in the future, so
another user who can write to the directory cannot make the logger
write, read, archive or delete a file through a link, block on a FIFO,
or stop its rotations.  An entry that matches the name but is not a
generation gets a "log rotation: ignored an entry that is not a
rotated file of this log: <name>" record at CLOG_WARN in the live file,
with every character outside printable ASCII, every quote and every
backslash written as '?'.  At most 8 of these records go out for a log
in any minute.

The 30-day limit has a cost when the system clock steps back by more
than 30 days.  A logger that stays open across the step keeps every
generation, because the limit counts from the newest name it made.  A
logger that opens the file after the step, the one of a restarted
process for example, sees the generations written before the step as
more than 30 days above both the clock and every name it made, so to
that logger they are not generations.  It never renames, compresses or
deletes them, they do not count toward cfg->max_rotated_files, and its
rotations name them in the CLOG_WARN record above.  That lasts until
the clock comes back within 30 days of their timestamps, and until then
the operator removes or renames them.

When path itself is a symbolic link, the logger writes through the link
until the first rotation, which moves the link to a rotated name.  From
then on the live file is a regular file that the logger made, and the
link, which is not a generation, stays where the rotation put it.

The timestamp and the collision number of each rotated name decide
which rotated files are the oldest: the names are ordered by the
timestamp and then by the number, compared as a number.  Every rotation
takes a name above all the names before it, so that order is the order
of the rotations, and the deletion always keeps the newest generations.
The modification time of a rotated file is not used, because the
compression step writes a compressed rotated file, and its modification
time tells when that compression finished, not how old the log content
inside it is.

The prune pass counts generations, one generation per rotation, so the
uncompressed file and the \&.gz file of one generation count once.  The
prune pass of a rotation never deletes the generation that the same
rotation has just created, but it still counts it against
cfg->max_rotated_files.  A prune pass also never deletes the generation
whose compression is running, and still counts it.  A pass keeps the
newest cfg->max_rotated_files generations and deletes every older one,
except an older one whose compression is running, which is deleted as
soon as that compression ends.  A generation that is still waiting for
its compression is deleted like any other once it is older than the
newest cfg->max_rotated_files.  So at most cfg->max_rotated_files
generations plus the one under compression stay on the disk.

When cfg->compress_rotated is true, each rotated file is
gzip-compressed with zlib after the rotation.  The compressed file goes
to <rotated-name>.gz, and after a success the original uncompressed
file is removed.  A compression thread of the logger does that work.
The first rotation that needs it starts the thread, and it compresses
one file at a time, the oldest first.

The rotation only puts the file in a queue, so the thread that logs,
the async writer thread and a producer behind a bounded async queue
never wait for gzip.  The last clog_close(3) of the file waits until
all queued compressions are complete, and a clog_fatal(3) record waits
for them within the budget of the exit drain below.

If the directory cannot hold the name <rotated-name>.gz.tmp (NAME_MAX,
255 bytes on common filesystems), no compression of that file can
succeed.  The file then stays uncompressed, and a "log rotation: a
rotated file stays uncompressed: ..." record at CLOG_WARN goes into the
new live file.

The standard gunzip(1) and zcat(1) tools read the \&.gz files that the
library makes.  A program that links the static library must also link
with -lz, which pkg-config --static --libs ccollections adds, while the
shared library has zlib as its own dependency.

A compression opens its source with O_NOFOLLOW and O_NONBLOCK and reads
it only when the descriptor is a regular file owned by the effective
user or by the owner of the live file, so it never reads through a link
and never waits on a FIFO or a device.
A compression writes its output to <rotated-name>.gz.tmp, flushes it to
the disk, and only then renames it to <rotated-name>.gz and removes the
uncompressed file, so a \&.gz file always holds a complete gzip stream,
even when the process stops in the middle of a compression.  The prune
pass and the naming of rotations never look at the \&.tmp file.  A
filesystem whose fsync(2) answers EINVAL or ENOSYS has no flush to
disk, and the compression goes ahead without it.  A compression never
replaces a file that already has the \&.gz name.  The \&.gz file gets
the permission bits of the file it compresses, as gzip(1) gives them,
and its owner and group under the same rule as the new live file of a
rotation.  A compression that fails removes its temporary output.  If
that removal fails too, as in a directory that lost its write
permission, the compression thread tries the removal again before its
next compression and when the logger closes, and the recovery at the
next open (below) removes whatever is still left.

When cfg->compress_rotated is true, the open finishes what an earlier
process left unfinished in the directory, for generations only.  It
removes every <rotated-name>.gz.tmp file of path that passes the same
test of type, owner and timestamp.  A \&.gz file beside its own
uncompressed file is not known to be complete, while the uncompressed
file holds everything, so the open removes that \&.gz file.  Then it
queues every uncompressed generation of path for compression, the
oldest first.  A generation whose \&.gz name holds an entry that is not
a generation stays uncompressed.

A process that ends with exit(3), or a return from main(), while the
logger is still open drains it within a budget of 5 seconds for the
whole process.  An exit handler that the library registers when it
loads delivers what an async logger still holds, as a clog_fatal(3)
record does, and waits for the queued compressions.  The budget also
bounds every lock that the drain takes, so a logger whose lock another
thread holds past the budget is skipped, and the records it still holds
can be lost.  Every write of the drain is bounded too, on a pipe, a
socket and a terminal as well; see clog_create_from_fd_mp(3).  A
compression that the budget cuts off stops, removes its \&.tmp output
and keeps the uncompressed file, and the next open of path compresses
it.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3) run no exit handler.

A file has one backing store in a process whenever a logger of it
rotates.  If a rotating logger of this process already writes the file,
or if this call rotates and a logger of this process already writes the
file, the call joins that logger exactly the way clog_derive(3) does:
the new handle has its own min_level and its own fields, and it shares
the output target, the format, the rotation and the async writer
thread.  The file counts as the same when the directory and the last
component of path are the same, however each call spells the directory
part.  Two different last components are two files, even when one of
them is a symbolic link to the other, so open a rotating log under one
name only.  The call returns CLOG_NULL instead when its rotation
configuration, its async configuration (NULL or not, and every resolved
value) or its allocator differs from that logger's, and also when one
of the two rotates and the other does not.  Two independent backing
stores on one rotating file would each rename, delete and compress
files that the other one still writes, and records would be lost.  Two
opens of one file that both have rotation off stay two independent
loggers, each appending whole records to the file.

The join covers the loggers of one process only.  Rotation renames,
deletes and compresses the files of the log, so two processes that
rotate one file each do that under the other one and lose records.
Give each process its own file (in a prefork server, for example, a
name that holds the worker number) or let one process write the log.
Two processes may append to one file with rotation off.
A call for a file whose last clog_close(3) is still running waits until
that close has finished.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
path       Path of the log file, copied by the call.
min_level  Messages below this level are silently dropped.
cfg        The rotation configuration, or NULL to turn rotation off.
           A max_file_size or max_rotated_files <= 0, and a
           rotation_interval_us of 0, fall back to their default,
           which clogger.h also names as a constant:
             size_rotation_enabled  - turns size rotation on
             max_file_size          - int64_t bytes threshold
                                      (<= 0 = CLOG_DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_SIZE,
                                      10 MiB)
             time_rotation_enabled  - turns time rotation on
             rotation_interval_us   - uint64_t microseconds
                                      (0 = CLOG_DEFAULT_ROTATION_INTERVAL_US,
                                      86400000000 = 1 day)
             max_rotated_files      - files kept
                                      (<= 0 = CLOG_DEFAULT_MAX_ROTATED_FILES,
                                      7)
             compress_rotated       - gzip-compress each rotated file
           max_file_size is a fixed-width int64_t and
           rotation_interval_us is a uint64_t, so the struct has one
           layout per target whatever _FILE_OFFSET_BITS and
           _TIME_BITS are in the including translation unit, and you
           need no particular compilation flag for it to mean the
           same thing on both sides of the library boundary.
async_cfg  The configuration for async logging, or NULL for
           synchronous logging.  When it is not NULL, a clog_<level>()
           call formats only its own message and hands the rest to a
           dedicated writer thread.  clog_create_from_fd_mp(3) lists
           all the fields.  Unlike cfg above, a struct of all zeros
           still turns async mode on, with a default for every zero
           field; only a literal NULL keeps the logging synchronous.
mprocs     A custom allocator with function pointers for malloc, free,
           calloc and realloc, or NULL for the default system
           allocator.  When mprocs is not NULL all four pointers must
           be set; a struct that is only partly filled makes the call
           return CLOG_NULL and no logger is created.  The library
           copies the struct, so you can free yours after the call.
           Every logger that clog_derive() makes from this one gets
           the same allocator.  The two allocations that belong to
           the library, the symbol text of a backtrace and the
           identity cache of each thread, use malloc(3); see
           clogger(7).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new logger handle on success.
CLOG_NULL after a failure.  That is a bad path, a permission error,
a min_level that is not one of the values of clog_level_t, an mprocs
that is not valid or a failed allocation; size rotation turned on when
the current size of an existing file cannot be found; a file that a
rotating logger of this process already writes with another
configuration; a rotating open of a file that a logger of this process
writes without rotation; or an open while the process is ending, after
the library has released its table of loggers, which is what an open
from a destructor that runs after the destructor of the library gets.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The logger owns the file descriptor and closes it on clog_close().
O_CLOEXEC is set on the file descriptor.
The rename and the open of the new fd happen inside the locked section,
so no log line is lost and no log line ends up between others across
the rotation boundary.
When compress_rotated is true, gzip runs on the compression thread of
the logger, after the new fd is already in use and outside the mutex,
so the I/O of the compression never stalls a log writer.
The directory descriptor of a rotating logger carries O_CLOEXEC, like
the file descriptor.
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with clogger:
  - call fork(2) before you open a logger;
  - open a logger, call fork(2), and then call exec(3) immediately.
A process that goes on without exec(3) while a handle from before the
fork is live is not using a supported pattern.

As a defence in depth, a handle that is open at the time of such a fork
can still be used in the child, and log, derive and close work
correctly on both sides.  The async writer thread of a logger does not
exist in a forked child, so an inherited handle with async mode on
switches to synchronous logging in the child, without the caller
noticing.

This fallback is part of the fork protection of the library, which a
build with -DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 leaves out, and such a process
must not fork while it holds a live async logger.

The compression thread does not exist in the child either.  The
compressions that the parent queued stay with the parent, and a later
rotation in the child starts a compression thread of its own.

When async_cfg is not NULL, a batch that the writer thread builds can
cover several records that the application logged moments apart, and
such a batch can cross a rotation boundary.  It is written to whichever
file is current at the time of the flush, instead of being split at
the exact moment each record was logged.

The kernel can accept only part of a write, for example when a
filesystem has just run out of space or an RLIMIT_FSIZE ceiling is
reached.  The bytes it did take then sit on the disk without the
terminating newline of that record.  A synchronous logger writes that
newline itself, so the next record starts on a line of its own and is
never joined onto the fragment, and then it writes a "log record
truncated: <n> of <m> bytes written" record that names the loss.  An
async logger keeps the bytes that the write did not take in its
batching buffer and offers them again on the next flush.  They are the
exact continuation of what did reach the disk, so once the condition
clears the logger completes the record and delivers every record behind
it.  Size rotation and time rotation both stand down while those bytes
are outstanding, so the two halves of a record always reach the same
file, and the flush that delivers the bytes also takes the rotation.  A
file with no room cannot take them, though, and the rotation is what
frees room, because it deletes the oldest generations.  So when the
write stopped with ENOSPC, EDQUOT or EFBIG and a rotation is due, the
flush gives up on the continuation, names it with a "log record
truncated: <n> undelivered bytes dropped" record, rotates, and offers
the records it kept to the new file at once.  A rotation never comes
between the two halves of a record that the logger still delivers.

A batch that a full disk held back can grow far past
cfg->max_file_size.  When the batch holds more than flush_buffer_size
bytes and would take the file past cfg->max_file_size, the flush writes
it in pieces that end on record boundaries and checks the size rotation
between the pieces, so each file grows past cfg->max_file_size by one
record at most.  An ordinary flush is a single write, so it can take
the file past cfg->max_file_size by up to flush_buffer_size bytes
before the size rotation that follows it.

The newline and the marker of a loss go to the output that has just
failed, and on a full disk they usually fail too.  The logger then
keeps both and writes them in front of the next write that the output
accepts.  An async logger also tries them at each flush of its writer
thread, so they don't wait for a new record, and every logger tries
them at exit(3) when nothing is written again: the newline first, then
one marker.  While a single loss waits, the marker carries its own
text; for more than one it reads "log record truncated: <k> earlier
losses (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>", with the number
of losses, the records and the bytes they lost, and the errno of the
last write that failed.  A record that arrives while that newline is
still waiting is not written, because it would continue the fragment on
the same line, and it counts as one more loss.  So every line of the
log holds at most one record, and every loss is named once the output
has room again.  An error that no retry gets past, such as EPIPE from a
pipe whose reader is gone, ends the wait: the output is gone, so the
logger gives up the newline and the marker.  The last clog_close(3) of
the logger makes one more attempt within 100 milliseconds, and a loss
that it cannot name then stays unnamed.

A rotation never leaves such a fragment at the end of a generation.  If
the newline is still owed when the logger rotates, the logger writes it
to the generation it retires, after the rotation has deleted the oldest
generations.  If that file still refuses it, for example because the
fragment reaches an RLIMIT_FSIZE ceiling, the logger cuts the file back
to the end of its last whole line, and the bytes that the cut removes
count toward the bytes that the marker names.  So every rotated
generation ends with a newline, and the marker that names the loss
starts the new file on a line of its own.

A flush keeps the undelivered bytes only after an error that a later
attempt can get past: ENOSPC, EDQUOT, EFBIG, ENOBUFS, ENOMEM, or EAGAIN
or EINTR that ends a wait.  Any other write error, such as EPIPE,
ECONNRESET or EBADF, fails the same way on every retry, so the flush
drops the undelivered bytes and writes a "log record truncated:
undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>"
record in their place, and the records after it go out as usual.  The
last flush of clog_close(3) has no later retry, so what it cannot
deliver is named the same way, with "the logger closed" as the reason.
A record that does not fit into the batching buffer behind the bytes
that a flush keeps is a loss as well, and a "log record truncated: a
record did not fit behind the undelivered batch (<n> byte message)"
record names it; the library never calls such a record too large,
because the record itself is small.

Some records have to reach the file before those bytes do, and the
library never writes such a record between the two halves of the
record that the bytes belong to.  There are two such cases, a
CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG record, which is never batched, and a record that takes
the synchronous write path.  Each of them first gives the outstanding
bytes one more delivery attempt of its own.  If the file still won't
take them, it gives up on them and on every remaining line of the split
record, backtrace lines included, and writes the missing newline of the
split record (when a line is open) and a "log record truncated: <n>
undelivered bytes dropped" record in their place.  A logfmt record
whose backtrace lines are still outstanding counts as split even when
the bytes already on the disk end with the newline of its first line.
The records that the batch holds behind the split one are kept, and a
later flush delivers them, so they follow the record that had to be
written past them.  Once the continuation is given up nothing is
outstanding, so the next check takes the rotation that stood down for
it, exactly as it would after a delivery.

A write to a regular file cannot raise SIGPIPE, but a write at the
RLIMIT_FSIZE limit of the process raises SIGXFSZ, and its default
disposition ends the process with a core dump before the repair above
can run.  Under the default policy, CLOG_SIGNALS_AUTO, the open (and
each rotation that opens the next file) therefore sets the disposition
of SIGXFSZ to SIG_IGN for the whole process, once, and only while it is
still SIG_DFL, so a handler or an ignore that the application installed
stays in place.  A write at the limit then fails with EFBIG and the
logger survives it.  The async writer thread and the compression thread
also keep SIGXFSZ blocked in their own signal masks.  Under
CLOG_SIGNALS_UNTOUCHED the library leaves SIGXFSZ alone, and an
application that sets a file size limit must ignore SIGXFSZ, or block
it in every thread that can write a log file; otherwise the kernel ends
the process once the log reaches the limit.  When path names a FIFO,
the logger treats it as a pipe.  clog_set_signal_policy(3) covers both
signals.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_close (3),
.BR clog_flush (3),
.BR clog_set_signal_policy (3),
.BR clog_fatal (3),
.BR clogger (7)
